00:11:54,070 --> 00:11:56,110
[SPEAKER_01]: The players and the manager?

214
00:11:56,130 --> 00:11:57,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, they'd give me a howl.

215
00:11:57,391 --> 00:11:58,351
[SPEAKER_02]: What the hell you doing here?

216
00:11:59,271 --> 00:12:00,811
[SPEAKER_02]: Can't help us.

217
00:12:06,913 --> 00:12:08,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you find that they

218
00:12:08,770 --> 00:12:15,211
[SPEAKER_01]: really go after you or is this just a... Well, Bill Huggins was the manager of the club in 1925.

219
00:12:15,231 --> 00:12:28,454
[SPEAKER_02]: I was at the front of the city office, you know how they do out there, the flies to the outfield.

220
00:12:29,194 --> 00:12:31,335
[SPEAKER_02]: He was sitting on a bench and I was over on that side.

221
00:12:33,475 --> 00:12:34,315
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, hey, Ruffin.

222
00:12:35,676 --> 00:12:36,936
[SPEAKER_02]: I turned around and he says, come here a minute.

223
00:12:38,443 --> 00:12:38,923
[SPEAKER_02]: So I went over.

224
00:12:40,284 --> 00:12:43,107
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, I'm going to make it a habit of talking to the visiting ball players.

225
00:12:44,548 --> 00:12:46,149
[SPEAKER_02]: At that time, I was playing the outfield, too.

226
00:12:46,169 --> 00:12:47,730
[SPEAKER_02]: So I was playing the outfield a few games.

227
00:12:48,551 --> 00:12:52,514
[SPEAKER_02]: So I guess he's going to try to make an outfield on me, because I get hit pretty good.

228
00:12:52,534 --> 00:12:55,016
[SPEAKER_02]: So he called me over.

229
00:12:55,036 --> 00:12:57,278
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, what are they trying to do, make an outfield out of you?

230
00:12:58,479 --> 00:12:59,099
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, I guess so.

231
00:12:59,139 --> 00:13:01,761
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, I can't win ball games until I get to try something else.

232
00:13:03,122 --> 00:13:05,164
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, you just tell them you don't want to play the outfield.

233
00:13:06,325 --> 00:13:06,785
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, why?

234
00:13:07,807 --> 00:13:10,328
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, you'll be over on this club next year.

235
00:13:10,348 --> 00:13:11,148
[SPEAKER_02]: So you just tell them.

236
00:13:11,808 --> 00:13:15,289
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know how the hell I'm going to tell this to Washington.

237
00:13:15,309 --> 00:13:19,549
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want to bring it out to them, because you can't do it anyway.

238
00:13:19,569 --> 00:13:21,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if they tell you to go, you've got to go.

239
00:13:25,751 --> 00:13:28,991
[SPEAKER_01]: So Huggins had his eye on you before the Yankees got you.

240
00:13:30,632 --> 00:13:31,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So the same year, he died.

241
00:13:31,932 --> 00:13:35,253
[SPEAKER_02]: So when he died, I said, there goes my trade.

242
00:13:35,273 --> 00:13:37,253
[SPEAKER_02]: There goes Ruffin from the Yankees.

243
00:13:39,052 --> 00:13:43,297
[SPEAKER_02]: It was all cut and dried, I guess, before he passed away.

244
00:13:45,620 --> 00:13:47,803
[SPEAKER_02]: All he had was a pimple on his face and scratches.

245
00:13:49,285 --> 00:13:49,965
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that what it did?

246
00:13:52,789 --> 00:13:53,670
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess not.

247
00:14:10,772 --> 00:14:14,173
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot of things really began to move up for you, didn't they?

248
00:14:14,193 --> 00:14:14,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

249
00:14:15,913 --> 00:14:18,694
[SPEAKER_01]: We had some good years.

250
00:14:18,814 --> 00:14:21,755
[SPEAKER_01]: In 1932 we won a pennant.

251
00:14:26,176 --> 00:14:29,396
[SPEAKER_01]: We won a game in the World Series, yeah?

252
00:14:29,416 --> 00:14:30,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember that game you won?

253
00:14:30,577 --> 00:14:31,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

254
00:14:33,497 --> 00:14:40,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Was it a close game?

255
00:14:41,736 --> 00:14:42,997
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I think it was 12-8.

256
00:14:48,859 --> 00:14:52,221
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Ruth's calling his shot that serious?

257
00:14:52,321 --> 00:14:53,981
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that was in Chicago.

258
00:14:54,501 --> 00:14:57,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he really call that in the event, or what were the circumstances?

259
00:14:57,763 --> 00:14:59,544
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, Charlie Rook, the pitcher, he said he didn't.

260
00:14:59,964 --> 00:15:01,124
[SPEAKER_02]: He said it was a damn lie.

261
00:15:03,225 --> 00:15:04,826
[SPEAKER_02]: But I don't think so.

262
00:15:04,846 --> 00:15:09,168
[SPEAKER_02]: He pointed right out to center field bleachers after they had two strikes on them.

263
00:15:09,188 --> 00:15:09,668
[SPEAKER_02]: Next pitch.

264
00:15:11,992 --> 00:15:20,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you remember Woody English?

265
00:15:21,517 --> 00:15:23,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

266
00:15:23,178 --> 00:15:24,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he pretty tough?

267
00:15:24,259 --> 00:15:25,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you remember him as a hitter?

268
00:15:25,780 --> 00:15:27,641
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he wasn't bad, he wasn't bad either.

269
00:15:29,002 --> 00:15:31,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Not the way they run them, the shortstops.

270
00:15:34,905 --> 00:15:37,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Who were some of the toughest hitters you faced after you got over the Yankees?

271
00:15:39,667 --> 00:15:40,868
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll tell you, they were all tough.

272
00:15:41,429 --> 00:15:41,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah?

273
00:15:48,495 --> 00:15:51,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Good luck with them though, according to the record here.

274
00:15:53,659 --> 00:15:54,580
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Earl Abram?

275
00:15:55,360 --> 00:15:55,841
[SPEAKER_01]: He was tough.

276
00:15:59,284 --> 00:16:02,807
[SPEAKER_01]: God, he's hitting that band box now, and there's only 250 feet to the right of the wall.

277
00:16:03,387 --> 00:16:05,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that a tough part to pitch in, would you say?

278
00:16:05,689 --> 00:16:07,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

279
00:16:07,030 --> 00:16:08,892
[SPEAKER_01]: You thinking about that wall when you're pitching?

280
00:16:08,912 --> 00:16:09,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

281
00:16:11,258 --> 00:16:12,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to keep it away from those guys, huh?

282
00:16:12,758 --> 00:16:18,200
[SPEAKER_02]: I was in one of those areas one day, and well, we were in together most of the time.

283
00:16:19,781 --> 00:16:21,181
[SPEAKER_02]: And I was in the Cleveland Hotel.

284
00:16:22,961 --> 00:16:26,142
[SPEAKER_02]: He went downstairs, and when he came back up, I was sitting in the clothes closet, see?

285
00:16:27,263 --> 00:16:29,423
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, what the hell are you doing there sitting in the clothes closet?

286
00:16:30,064 --> 00:16:35,125
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, I got a pitch today, so when I go out that damn band box, I look like the Yankee Stadium team.

287
00:16:36,305 --> 00:16:38,026
[SPEAKER_02]: I got such a kick out of it.

288
00:16:39,979 --> 00:16:42,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that a pretty happy team, those Yankees?

289
00:16:43,021 --> 00:16:45,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Jokes and good relations?

290
00:16:46,704 --> 00:16:46,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

291
00:16:48,306 --> 00:16:51,068
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Joe McCarthy?

292
00:16:51,088 --> 00:16:53,330
[SPEAKER_01]: He was all right if he wasn't drinking.

293
00:16:53,350 --> 00:16:53,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah?

294
00:16:55,992 --> 00:16:57,293
[SPEAKER_01]: What did Shockey go for?

295
00:16:58,834 --> 00:16:59,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Remember?

296
00:16:59,234 --> 00:17:09,062
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, Shockey was just there about a year, I guess, wasn't he, or two, and then McCarthy came in, and I wondered what Shockey... wasn't he a good manager or...

297
00:17:09,567 --> 00:17:10,207
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess not.

298
00:17:10,227 --> 00:17:14,928
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why I let him go, because he brought Joe McArthur over next year, and next year he won the pennant.

299
00:17:17,729 --> 00:17:20,050
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess if you win, that makes you a good manager, huh?

300
00:17:21,330 --> 00:17:21,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

301
00:17:22,210 --> 00:17:23,851
[SPEAKER_02]: You've got to win a pennant to be a manager.

302
00:17:26,492 --> 00:17:29,072
[SPEAKER_01]: If you win a pennant, it seems like you always get a job.

303
00:17:30,773 --> 00:17:33,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you think managing is that important for you?

304
00:17:33,394 --> 00:17:33,634
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

305
00:17:34,854 --> 00:17:37,175
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems like if you have good material, you're going to win, though.

306
00:17:37,195 --> 00:17:37,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

307
00:17:40,285 --> 00:17:46,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Casey Stengel, he couldn't do much with the Boston Braves, but the Yankees.

308
00:17:49,593 --> 00:17:53,737
[SPEAKER_01]: In 1933, you had a kind of a bad year.

309
00:17:54,418 --> 00:17:58,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have a bad arm that year or something?

310
00:17:58,501 --> 00:17:59,222
[SPEAKER_01]: 1914, I'd say.

311
00:17:59,242 --> 00:18:01,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the last year I had a bad shoulder.

312
00:18:03,686 --> 00:18:05,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you came back real well in 1934.

313
00:18:09,453 --> 00:18:10,734
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Lou Gehrig?

314
00:18:11,234 --> 00:18:16,198
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a good hard-nosed player.

315
00:18:20,941 --> 00:18:23,583
[SPEAKER_02]: Gehrig's more of a serious type than very ruthless.

316
00:18:28,666 --> 00:18:29,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Bob Musil, a pretty...

317
00:18:30,868 --> 00:18:33,630
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a good officer.

318
00:18:33,650 --> 00:18:37,953
[SPEAKER_02]: He's a terrific one.

319
00:18:46,773 --> 00:18:52,555
[SPEAKER_02]: Ordinary five balls you see now, left fielders get eyes on the turn, calling home.

320
00:18:53,275 --> 00:18:54,195
[SPEAKER_02]: Wouldn't do that on Musial.

321
00:18:55,195 --> 00:18:55,895
[SPEAKER_02]: He'd throw you out.

322
00:18:56,556 --> 00:18:58,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Ruth would too, wouldn't he?

323
00:18:58,116 --> 00:18:58,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Ruth would do it.

324
00:18:59,376 --> 00:19:00,377
[SPEAKER_01]: That would sum it up for you.

325
00:19:00,397 --> 00:19:01,277
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Earl Combs?

326
00:19:01,297 --> 00:19:02,077
[SPEAKER_01]: He had pretty good arms?

327
00:19:02,097 --> 00:19:02,357
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

328
00:19:03,237 --> 00:19:09,999
[SPEAKER_02]: In fact, Combs has a stroke too.

329
00:19:10,019 --> 00:19:11,260
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, is that right?

330
00:19:11,540 --> 00:19:12,460
[SPEAKER_02]: Can't use his arms.

331
00:19:15,238 --> 00:19:16,479
[SPEAKER_01]: I hope to get down to see him.

332
00:19:16,499 --> 00:19:19,600
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't live too far from where we are, in Kentucky there.

333
00:19:19,660 --> 00:19:20,901
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he's in, what's his name?

334
00:19:20,921 --> 00:19:21,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Richmond.

335
00:19:22,042 --> 00:19:25,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Richmond, yeah.

336
00:19:25,404 --> 00:19:32,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we were at a meeting last spring up in Monterey on a... Somebody read a paper on Babe Ruth.

337
00:19:32,948 --> 00:19:37,110
[SPEAKER_01]: A fellow had written a paper on him, and he had interviewed Earl Combs once or twice.

338
00:19:38,191 --> 00:19:42,413
[SPEAKER_01]: I think this was before he had his stroke, but he had a nice visit down there.

339
00:19:47,768 --> 00:19:49,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Who were some of your other picks?

340
00:19:49,049 --> 00:19:53,311
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Lefty Gomez as a teammate and pitcher?

341
00:19:53,571 --> 00:19:54,292
[SPEAKER_01]: He was all right.

342
00:19:54,312 --> 00:19:55,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Lefty and I roomed together.

343
00:19:55,773 --> 00:20:00,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he pretty much of a prankster and pulling jokes all the time?

344
00:20:00,975 --> 00:20:04,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he didn't really love that.

345
00:20:04,317 --> 00:20:04,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Not too much.

346
00:20:05,938 --> 00:20:08,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he as witty, as humorous as they said?

347
00:20:08,239 --> 00:20:08,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

348
00:20:10,161 --> 00:20:15,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeps everybody loosened up, huh?

349
00:20:17,973 --> 00:20:19,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he was quite a kid.

350
00:20:23,656 --> 00:20:25,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever get thrown out of any games?

351
00:20:25,657 --> 00:20:25,837
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

352
00:20:27,578 --> 00:20:30,339
[SPEAKER_01]: I tried to, but the umpires wouldn't throw me out.

353
00:20:30,459 --> 00:20:31,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Why'd you try to?

354
00:20:34,581 --> 00:20:39,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it was definitely between lefty and I. Lefty used to pitch, and I managed to let him off the next day.

355
00:20:39,184 --> 00:20:40,344
[SPEAKER_02]: So he'd take his wife to the beach.

356
00:20:42,766 --> 00:20:45,667
[SPEAKER_02]: And I went to MacArthur one day, I says,

357
00:20:46,652 --> 00:20:50,473
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, I'm married, I could use a damn day off, too.

358
00:20:51,574 --> 00:20:52,834
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, we need you for pinch hitting.

359
00:20:54,175 --> 00:20:56,655
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, no, it's a lot of crap, Joe.

360
00:20:56,675 --> 00:20:57,436
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, no, it isn't.

361
00:20:57,896 --> 00:20:59,156
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, you're a team man, aren't you?

362
00:20:59,196 --> 00:20:59,757
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, yes.

363
00:21:00,777 --> 00:21:06,059
[SPEAKER_02]: So I decided my only way I'm going to get a day off is I'm going to Sunday a close play.

364
00:21:07,059 --> 00:21:08,099
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm going to get on the umpire.

365
00:21:09,140 --> 00:21:10,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Make him throw me out of the game.

366
00:21:15,112 --> 00:21:19,114
[SPEAKER_02]: So what happened one day on a close play at third base, I started riding the umpire.

367
00:21:20,775 --> 00:21:22,015
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, I know you're kidding me.

368
00:21:22,035 --> 00:21:23,936
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, I'm not kidding you.

369
00:21:23,956 --> 00:21:25,157
[SPEAKER_02]: You're blind, so-and-so.

370
00:21:26,097 --> 00:21:29,579
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, referee, if you think I'm going to throw you out of the game so you can go to the beach, you're crazy.

371
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:32,220
[SPEAKER_02]: You've got to stay here and suffer with me.

372
00:21:34,241 --> 00:21:35,342
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, thanks so much.

373
00:21:36,102 --> 00:21:36,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Who was that?

374
00:21:36,663 --> 00:21:37,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you remember?

375
00:21:37,563 --> 00:21:39,404
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know.

376
00:21:48,241 --> 00:21:49,302
[SPEAKER_00]: When did you get married?

377
00:21:50,783 --> 00:21:51,084
[SPEAKER_02]: When?

378
00:21:51,964 --> 00:21:52,225
[SPEAKER_02]: 1934.

379
00:21:52,245 --> 00:22:02,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you meet your wife through some of the baseball connections?

380
00:22:02,373 --> 00:22:05,536
[SPEAKER_01]: No, just my hometown.

381
00:22:06,777 --> 00:22:08,119
[SPEAKER_01]: What did you do in the winter times?

382
00:22:08,759 --> 00:22:09,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

383
00:22:09,780 --> 00:22:10,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you go back home and go hunting?

384
00:22:11,522 --> 00:22:13,123
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I used to do a little hunting, but not much.

385
00:22:19,150 --> 00:22:27,695
[SPEAKER_02]: too hard on my foot, I couldn't walk through those rough terrain, you know.

386
00:22:27,715 --> 00:22:29,216
[SPEAKER_01]: You mean where you lost the toes?

387
00:22:31,658 --> 00:22:36,060
[SPEAKER_01]: How did that affect your pitching?

388
00:22:36,181 --> 00:22:38,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that where you put the weight on the foot there?

389
00:22:38,602 --> 00:22:42,204
[SPEAKER_01]: I put it on the side.

390
00:22:42,424 --> 00:22:46,047
[SPEAKER_02]: Land on my side and then flop over on it.

391
00:22:48,591 --> 00:23:01,139
[SPEAKER_02]: I hit like that, and then came like that.

392
00:23:01,159 --> 00:23:03,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Very special shoot, let's say.

393
00:23:08,183 --> 00:23:09,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Didn't bother hitting your pitching, did it?

394
00:23:10,425 --> 00:23:10,825
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

395
00:23:11,906 --> 00:23:14,327
[SPEAKER_01]: How many home runs did you hit in your career, do you remember?

396
00:23:14,347 --> 00:23:15,788
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know what's wrong.

397
00:23:17,705 --> 00:23:31,856
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something I say, I didn't know how many home runs I hit or anything.

398
00:23:33,877 --> 00:23:40,943
[SPEAKER_02]: I was sitting and talking one day, and we had a statistician there from, what, the ball club of Cleveland years ago?

399
00:23:42,024 --> 00:23:43,024
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, he was a shootie.

400
00:23:45,648 --> 00:23:47,769
[SPEAKER_02]: He was terrific.

401
00:23:48,609 --> 00:23:58,254
[SPEAKER_02]: You know all about him.

402
00:23:59,354 --> 00:23:59,874
[SPEAKER_02]: Who was that?

403
00:24:00,555 --> 00:24:03,816
[SPEAKER_02]: I forget his name, but he hung himself.

404
00:24:04,736 --> 00:24:05,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah?

405
00:24:05,377 --> 00:24:06,237
[SPEAKER_02]: He said he had a brain.

406
00:24:06,257 --> 00:24:07,518
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, that's what happens.

407
00:24:07,618 --> 00:24:08,438
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he's too bright.

408
00:24:08,638 --> 00:24:09,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Too bright.

409
00:24:10,019 --> 00:24:11,139
[SPEAKER_02]: Hung himself the first time.

410
00:24:13,849 --> 00:24:21,773
[SPEAKER_02]: He talked to me one day, he says, do you know you have a record in who's who after being the best hitting pitcher in baseball?

411
00:24:21,793 --> 00:24:22,614
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, no, I don't.

412
00:24:24,155 --> 00:24:28,637
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, you hit over 300 seven years in a row in a pitcher?

413
00:24:29,738 --> 00:24:30,238
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, no.

414
00:24:30,258 --> 00:24:31,999
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, what's in who's who?

415
00:24:33,580 --> 00:24:39,123
[SPEAKER_02]: I never knew it, but he did.

416
00:24:39,163 --> 00:24:42,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you wanted to know these things when it came contract time, didn't you?

417
00:24:43,225 --> 00:24:43,705
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't help.

418
00:24:45,915 --> 00:24:46,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever hold out any?

419
00:24:48,135 --> 00:24:48,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Every year.

420
00:24:49,816 --> 00:24:50,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Every year.

421
00:24:51,276 --> 00:24:51,896
[SPEAKER_01]: For how long?

422
00:24:53,296 --> 00:24:54,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, one time I held out till May.

423
00:24:54,857 --> 00:24:55,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that right?

424
00:24:55,657 --> 00:24:56,537
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the Yankees, huh?

425
00:24:58,337 --> 00:25:00,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Who'd you negotiate your contracts with?

426
00:25:00,398 --> 00:25:01,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Ed Barrow?

427
00:25:01,198 --> 00:25:02,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Ed Barrow.

428
00:25:02,358 --> 00:25:03,398
[SPEAKER_01]: How was he to deal with?

429
00:25:03,438 --> 00:25:03,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Tough.

430
00:25:04,038 --> 00:25:04,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I bet he was.

431
00:25:07,879 --> 00:25:10,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you each give in a little bit then before you finally signed?

432
00:25:11,940 --> 00:25:14,320
[SPEAKER_02]: We'd have to trust each other out over the phone sometimes.

433
00:25:16,931 --> 00:25:21,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Did it take you long to get in shape in the spring when you held out like that, or did you keep in shape?

434
00:25:21,255 --> 00:25:23,637
[SPEAKER_02]: I kept in shape, yeah, I lived in California at that time.

435
00:25:24,577 --> 00:25:27,140
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm out there to play ball all year round.

436
00:25:28,140 --> 00:25:30,002
[SPEAKER_02]: There's always somebody working on it, you know.

437
00:25:30,222 --> 00:25:31,843
[SPEAKER_01]: It really wasn't a problem getting in shape.

438
00:25:31,923 --> 00:25:39,250
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, I lived in Long Beach and had a ballpark down there, and every Sunday they played ball games there, so I used to be out every Sunday and work out.

439
00:25:40,330 --> 00:25:43,013
[SPEAKER_02]: When I got my arm in pretty good shape, I pitched about four or five innings.

440
00:25:44,014 --> 00:25:45,415
[SPEAKER_02]: When I got to spring training, I was ready.

441
00:25:47,740 --> 00:25:51,162
[SPEAKER_02]: I was embarrassed with that as well as anybody else because they had their scouts out there.

442
00:25:51,182 --> 00:25:52,282
[SPEAKER_02]: They watched every ball game.

443
00:25:52,322 --> 00:25:59,205
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't tell them New York, you know, they'd call up, say Ruffin's working out of his pitch and he's in pretty good shape.

444
00:25:59,705 --> 00:26:00,746
[SPEAKER_02]: So they never worried about me.

445
00:26:00,846 --> 00:26:02,066
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm out of hell out.

446
00:26:02,086 --> 00:26:03,407
[SPEAKER_02]: They figured I'd be ready to go.

447
00:26:12,391 --> 00:26:13,631
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember you had it so hard.

448
00:26:14,432 --> 00:26:15,312
[SPEAKER_02]: I was out playing golf.

449
00:26:16,216 --> 00:26:17,797
[SPEAKER_02]: The heavy Yankees carried out California.

450
00:26:17,817 --> 00:26:19,518
[SPEAKER_02]: He walked around the whole 18 holes with me.

451
00:26:20,759 --> 00:26:23,020
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want my arm, kept asking about my arm and that.

452
00:26:23,981 --> 00:26:25,402
[SPEAKER_02]: Did I feel good and all that stuff?

453
00:26:26,222 --> 00:26:26,963
[SPEAKER_02]: I know what he's up to.

454
00:26:26,983 --> 00:26:30,565
[SPEAKER_02]: If I'd have said, yo, my arm's still hurting, I'd have been treated.

455
00:26:31,405 --> 00:26:31,946
[SPEAKER_02]: Next day.

456
00:26:33,987 --> 00:26:36,308
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't know they checked up on them that way.

457
00:26:36,489 --> 00:26:38,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

458
00:26:38,710 --> 00:26:39,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Sneaky.

459
00:26:40,551 --> 00:26:41,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Sneaky way of doing it, but they do it.

460
00:26:43,564 --> 00:26:45,105
[SPEAKER_01]: But did you move to California then?

461
00:26:45,465 --> 00:26:47,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Or were you just spending some time there?

462
00:26:47,745 --> 00:26:50,046
[SPEAKER_01]: No, when I got married, my wife and I went out there.

463
00:26:50,066 --> 00:26:50,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see.

464
00:26:51,987 --> 00:26:54,648
[SPEAKER_01]: You had been living in Illinois all these years?

465
00:26:57,849 --> 00:26:59,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you live near Jim Bottomley and all?

466
00:26:59,869 --> 00:27:01,650
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, Jim and I from the same town.

467
00:27:01,730 --> 00:27:02,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there?

468
00:27:02,210 --> 00:27:04,031
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought he was from Illinois.

469
00:27:05,431 --> 00:27:06,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Sonny Jim, huh?

470
00:27:06,972 --> 00:27:09,413
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been working on him to get him in the Hall of Fame for years.

471
00:27:09,653 --> 00:27:10,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he succeeded.

472
00:27:12,083 --> 00:27:12,663
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something.

473
00:27:12,863 --> 00:27:14,805
[SPEAKER_01]: A little town like that with two Hall of Famers.

474
00:27:18,006 --> 00:27:23,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's only 1,200 people in it.

475
00:27:23,409 --> 00:27:24,950
[SPEAKER_02]: The street had a lot of room there.

476
00:27:24,970 --> 00:27:29,313
[SPEAKER_02]: You had these cow fields, and they used to make ball downs out of them.

477
00:27:29,333 --> 00:27:32,555
[SPEAKER_02]: They'd all put bases down and go over there and play.

478
00:27:35,937 --> 00:27:39,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you and he play as kids or about the same age?

479
00:27:39,098 --> 00:27:39,899
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

480
00:27:40,419 --> 00:27:41,620
[SPEAKER_02]: Jimmy owned up before I did.

481
00:27:46,418 --> 00:27:49,380
[SPEAKER_02]: Matter of fact, that's the only time we got to know each other, when he's in a big loose house.

482
00:27:49,620 --> 00:27:57,746
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you ever pitch against him?

483
00:27:58,546 --> 00:27:58,826
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

484
00:28:12,642 --> 00:28:15,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And they'd get rings all the time, no other days at all.

485
00:28:15,963 --> 00:28:20,423
[SPEAKER_00]: Never a regular one.

486
00:28:24,184 --> 00:28:25,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, Joe DiMaggio came up.

487
00:28:26,224 --> 00:28:26,904
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Joe?

488
00:28:27,185 --> 00:28:27,465
[SPEAKER_01]: 1936.

489
00:28:27,485 --> 00:28:30,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Nice boy.

490
00:28:33,506 --> 00:28:34,686
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a ball player of the day.

491
00:28:34,706 --> 00:28:35,486
[SPEAKER_02]: He stepped in camp.

492
00:28:37,366 --> 00:28:41,347
[SPEAKER_01]: He could do it all, I guess.

493
00:28:42,484 --> 00:28:43,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You do.

494
00:28:44,205 --> 00:28:47,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Was he a pretty good team player?

495
00:28:48,027 --> 00:28:56,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

496
00:28:57,453 --> 00:28:59,314
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Ruth when Ruth left the team?

497
00:28:59,354 --> 00:29:04,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Was there some kind of decline in spirit, do you think?

498
00:29:04,417 --> 00:29:04,877
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

499
00:29:05,637 --> 00:29:07,478
[SPEAKER_01]: He was pretty well through then, I guess.

500
00:29:07,639 --> 00:29:10,100
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he's all washed out.

501
00:29:16,570 --> 00:29:38,339
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, talking with so many of these, the ones I go along on for the ride, these old ballplayers, it seems to me, of course this is just my view, I don't know anything, that the players today don't seem to have nearly the same attitude or team spirit that they used to.

502
00:29:46,448 --> 00:29:47,748
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd probably get $250,000 a year.

503
00:29:47,828 --> 00:29:49,469
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd probably get $24,000 a year.

504
00:29:51,210 --> 00:29:59,632
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that your top figure in the late 30s there?

505
00:29:59,652 --> 00:30:01,593
[SPEAKER_02]: You started winning 21, 20 games over a year.

506
00:30:02,593 --> 00:30:06,174
[SPEAKER_01]: How many 20-game years did you have?

507
00:30:06,254 --> 00:30:06,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Four.

508
00:30:22,603 --> 00:30:23,603
[SPEAKER_01]: What was your best pitch?

509
00:30:24,504 --> 00:30:25,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Pass ball.

510
00:30:27,827 --> 00:30:30,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Pass ball, I mixed it up with a change of pace.

511
00:30:31,129 --> 00:30:32,891
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a picture of you in this book here.

512
00:30:33,371 --> 00:30:39,116
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the 39th edition.

513
00:30:39,136 --> 00:30:44,780
[SPEAKER_01]: You had 21 and 7, didn't you?

514
00:30:49,145 --> 00:31:00,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You struck out about 120 strikeouts a year or so, and you averaged about 70 or so walks.

515
00:31:00,791 --> 00:31:02,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty good ratio, isn't it?

516
00:31:04,413 --> 00:31:07,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, which a lot of them are, you know, intentionally.

517
00:31:10,376 --> 00:31:17,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And you pitched over, boy, you went over 250 inning zones every year there for many years.

518
00:31:17,400 --> 00:31:18,240
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a lot of innings.

519
00:31:19,025 --> 00:31:24,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you ever pitch 300 innings a season?

520
00:31:25,027 --> 00:31:25,547
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

521
00:31:25,647 --> 00:31:26,247
[SPEAKER_01]: I wouldn't know.

522
00:31:29,408 --> 00:31:33,009
[SPEAKER_02]: See, in our day, they didn't know what they were doing.

523
00:31:33,029 --> 00:31:36,750
[SPEAKER_02]: Today, everything you do is a record.

524
00:31:36,790 --> 00:31:37,790
[SPEAKER_02]: You get a hangnail.

525
00:31:37,810 --> 00:31:39,331
[SPEAKER_02]: You went to the clubhouse and got it cut off.

526
00:31:40,351 --> 00:31:41,171
[SPEAKER_02]: It had to be in the paper.

527
00:31:41,191 --> 00:31:42,512
[SPEAKER_02]: You get a picture and everything in the paper.

528
00:31:42,552 --> 00:31:43,252
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not a hangnail.

529
00:31:46,944 --> 00:31:48,885
[SPEAKER_02]: If I saw him, I'd have paid for it myself.

530
00:31:49,605 --> 00:31:52,345
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd have to fix it.

531
00:31:52,365 --> 00:31:52,926
[SPEAKER_02]: They get up.

532
00:31:52,946 --> 00:31:53,906
[SPEAKER_02]: They take care of it.

533
00:32:00,628 --> 00:32:01,868
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember I was going to a World Series.

534
00:32:01,888 --> 00:32:03,048
[SPEAKER_02]: We played Cincinnati.

535
00:32:03,088 --> 00:32:06,749
[SPEAKER_02]: That year, I beat Paul Garrett at 2-1.

536
00:32:08,069 --> 00:32:08,710
[SPEAKER_02]: I saw him.

537
00:32:08,750 --> 00:32:09,930
[SPEAKER_02]: I couldn't move my arm.

538
00:32:10,010 --> 00:32:11,030
[SPEAKER_02]: I couldn't move my shoulder.

539
00:32:12,210 --> 00:32:14,291
[SPEAKER_02]: So I told McCarthy, I said, I can't open it.

540
00:32:15,252 --> 00:32:16,532
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, why not?

541
00:32:17,813 --> 00:32:19,993
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, I got a sore shoulder.

542
00:32:20,993 --> 00:32:23,534
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, oh, for Christ's sake.

543
00:32:23,554 --> 00:32:24,534
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't get a word, see?

544
00:32:25,494 --> 00:32:27,214
[SPEAKER_02]: He calls in a head scout from New York.

545
00:32:27,234 --> 00:32:30,875
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, you know any doctor around here that knows anything about arms?

546
00:32:31,815 --> 00:32:33,076
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, yeah, I know a guy in Brooklyn.

547
00:32:34,656 --> 00:32:36,296
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, he wants $25 for treatment.

548
00:32:36,316 --> 00:32:39,257
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, get him over here.

549
00:32:40,497 --> 00:32:41,237
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, I got to work, though.

550
00:32:41,257 --> 00:32:42,437
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm going to have to pay for it myself.

551
00:32:42,537 --> 00:32:43,338
[SPEAKER_02]: It's kind of a club thing.

552
00:32:45,832 --> 00:32:49,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Hey, you think they'd get away with that today?

553
00:32:51,833 --> 00:32:52,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Never.

554
00:32:52,933 --> 00:32:55,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he fix your arm or loosen it up a little?

555
00:32:55,314 --> 00:32:55,994
[SPEAKER_02]: He loosened it up.

556
00:32:56,074 --> 00:32:57,154
[SPEAKER_02]: He got it loose pretty good.

557
00:32:57,534 --> 00:32:58,594
[SPEAKER_02]: And you went out and pitched, huh?

558
00:32:58,654 --> 00:33:00,235
[SPEAKER_02]: I pitched a big game, two to one.

559
00:33:02,315 --> 00:33:05,956
[SPEAKER_02]: And I went up to the front office and I said, how about my money for my arm?

560
00:33:07,356 --> 00:33:09,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Boy, you saved your own arm by doing that.

561
00:33:10,456 --> 00:33:11,817
[SPEAKER_02]: We don't pay for that, Bearer says.

562
00:33:11,837 --> 00:33:12,237
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that Bearer?

563
00:33:12,257 --> 00:33:13,777
[SPEAKER_02]: Boy, he was tough.

564
00:33:15,182 --> 00:33:19,005
[SPEAKER_02]: He wouldn't give his mother a dollar, I don't think.

565
00:33:19,025 --> 00:33:22,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And now they pay for everything, operations, hospitalization.

566
00:33:24,430 --> 00:33:25,571
[SPEAKER_01]: All the fringe benefits.

567
00:33:26,732 --> 00:33:27,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they just fall.

568
00:33:28,093 --> 00:33:30,955
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it shows in the game.

569
00:33:31,636 --> 00:33:33,997
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that's why interest is falling off in baseball.

570
00:33:34,017 --> 00:33:34,358
[SPEAKER_00]: That's right.

571
00:33:36,460 --> 00:33:38,221
[SPEAKER_02]: They didn't go out there, they don't put their soul into it.

572
00:33:42,127 --> 00:33:46,070
[SPEAKER_01]: It seemed like the older players, this was their life.

573
00:33:47,251 --> 00:33:52,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And some of these younger players, it's a stepping stone to something else.

574
00:33:52,975 --> 00:34:00,359
[SPEAKER_01]: They just don't have the luck of the game, apparently.

575
00:34:00,399 --> 00:34:05,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you know, they asked me one time, what's the difference of pitching today and in your day?

576
00:34:05,903 --> 00:34:07,464
[SPEAKER_02]: I says, a lot of difference.

577
00:34:09,125 --> 00:34:10,426
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you think?

578
00:34:11,399 --> 00:34:12,400
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's how I figured.

579
00:34:13,461 --> 00:34:16,783
[SPEAKER_02]: Today, you go out there and pitch a pretty good ballgame.

580
00:34:16,863 --> 00:34:20,025
[SPEAKER_02]: I tell the writer, well, I thought I pitched pretty good today.

581
00:34:20,065 --> 00:34:23,128
[SPEAKER_02]: And on the other day, I said, we had a win.

582
00:34:24,169 --> 00:34:25,970
[SPEAKER_02]: We didn't say we thought we pitched a good game.

583
00:34:26,470 --> 00:34:27,311
[SPEAKER_02]: We didn't get paid for that.

584
00:34:27,351 --> 00:34:28,172
[SPEAKER_02]: We got paid to win.

585
00:34:28,192 --> 00:34:28,872
[SPEAKER_02]: So we had a win.

586
00:34:30,453 --> 00:34:32,395
[SPEAKER_02]: One night, I said, oh, I thought I did pretty good.

587
00:34:33,435 --> 00:34:34,396
[SPEAKER_02]: Either you win or you don't.

588
00:34:34,676 --> 00:34:35,217
[SPEAKER_02]: You're gone.

589
00:34:38,859 --> 00:34:40,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they get saved today.

590
00:34:41,217 --> 00:34:42,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, saves.

591
00:34:42,758 --> 00:34:44,078
[SPEAKER_01]: They count up as much as victories.

592
00:34:44,238 --> 00:34:45,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Just for a kickoff game, saves.

593
00:34:48,500 --> 00:34:53,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he may have a record of two wins and three defeats, but he's got 12 saves.

594
00:34:53,743 --> 00:34:55,224
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a great year.

595
00:34:57,705 --> 00:35:02,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Mike Marshall and all his saves.

596
00:35:03,127 --> 00:35:07,609
[SPEAKER_01]: How often did they, when you went out to pitch, you were supposed to pitch a whole game, weren't you?

597
00:35:07,970 --> 00:35:09,710
[SPEAKER_01]: They didn't tell you to go out and pitch five good innings.

598
00:35:16,987 --> 00:35:19,889
[SPEAKER_01]: But now they have these long relievers and short relievers.

599
00:35:21,370 --> 00:35:22,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you get reliever?

600
00:35:22,650 --> 00:35:23,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they knock you out much?

601
00:35:23,771 --> 00:35:24,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Not much, not much.

602
00:35:25,312 --> 00:35:26,393
[SPEAKER_01]: I'd do it all.

603
00:35:27,293 --> 00:35:28,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, you were different.

604
00:35:28,234 --> 00:35:28,854
[SPEAKER_01]: You could hit.

605
00:35:28,874 --> 00:35:29,895
[SPEAKER_01]: I could hit and it wouldn't take long.

606
00:35:29,915 --> 00:35:31,536
[SPEAKER_02]: They'd keep you in the game more because of that.

607
00:35:31,556 --> 00:35:33,978
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you think of the designator-hitter idea?

608
00:35:35,299 --> 00:35:37,140
[SPEAKER_02]: I'd have a good time in this day and age, wouldn't I?

609
00:35:37,700 --> 00:35:39,421
[SPEAKER_02]: You'd still be...

610
00:35:47,510 --> 00:35:48,991
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Yankee Stadium to pitch in?

611
00:35:49,692 --> 00:35:49,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Good.

612
00:35:50,793 --> 00:35:51,373
[SPEAKER_02]: All along?

613
00:35:52,774 --> 00:35:55,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Kept them up there and let them have it tomorrow, Joe.

614
00:35:56,057 --> 00:35:57,939
[SPEAKER_02]: See them go up over their heads and say, go get it, Joe.

615
00:36:00,260 --> 00:36:01,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have any favorite ballparks?

616
00:36:02,282 --> 00:36:03,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe that was it.

617
00:36:03,063 --> 00:36:03,363
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

618
00:36:04,744 --> 00:36:05,785
[SPEAKER_02]: I could run in Detroit.

619
00:36:07,206 --> 00:36:09,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Seemed like Detroit to me, but I could beat anything.

620
00:36:11,029 --> 00:36:12,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you killed Cleveland.

621
00:36:12,990 --> 00:36:14,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Boy, I used to go down there and see.

622
00:36:14,390 --> 00:36:16,911
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw my grub out there and see my Cleveland beat.

623
00:36:17,572 --> 00:36:19,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I didn't like you in those days.

624
00:36:19,412 --> 00:36:21,693
[SPEAKER_01]: I was a Cleveland fan.

625
00:36:25,635 --> 00:36:26,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Just killing all the time.

626
00:36:30,377 --> 00:36:32,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But you say some of their hitters gave you trouble, like Averill.

627
00:36:32,998 --> 00:36:33,778
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Trosky?

